    <id>https://doi.org/10.1177/01492063261465642</id>
    <title>The Impact of Strategic Leaders’ International Experience on Organizations: An Integrative Review and Multilevel Framework</title>
    <updated>2026-07-27T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Fei Qin, Klaus E. Meyer, Sabina Tasheva Nielsen</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Journal of Management&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;Internationally experienced business leaders bring distinct human capital, social capital, and cognitive schemas into organizations, influence strategic orientation through their decision-making, and in turn shape organizations’ strategies and performance. The impact of the international experience (IE) of an organization’s upper echelon has been studied in various bodies of management literature, varying in terms of the theoretical perspectives, conceptualizations of the IE construct, and outcomes considered. However, the effects are not straightforward with regard to which elements of IE affect which aspects of organizational dynamics, and how. This review integrates separate streams of organizational research to develop a more holistic understanding of the role of the IE held by strategic leaders such as chief executive officers, entrepreneurial founders, top management teams, and boards of directors, and its impact on organizational strategies and performance. We refine the conceptualization of the IE construct at multiple levels of analysis and explore cross-level mechanisms suggested by multiple theories to develop an integrative framework that links strategic leaders’ IE to organizational decisions, strategies, and performance. On this basis, we propose an agenda for future research.</content>
    <link href="https://doi.org/10.1177/01492063261465642"/>
    <category term="Journal of Management"/>
    <published>2026-07-27T00:00:00+00:00</published>
  </entry>
  <entry>
    <id>https://doi.org/10.1287/isre.2024.1300</id>
    <title>The Interplay of Opinions and Behavior in Social Trading: The Dynamics of Early Cryptocurrency Adoption</title>
    <updated>2026-07-27T00:00:00+00:00</updated>
    <author>
      <name>Ye Liu, Mingwen Yang, Matthias Pelster, Yong Tan</name>
    </author>
    <content>&lt;b&gt;Information Systems Research&lt;/b&gt; &lt;br&gt;This paper studies how the interplay of posting and trading behaviors within a social network influences an individual’s decision to adopt cryptocurrency. We exploit a unique dataset from a large social trading platform and find a pronounced social learning effect in cryptocurrency adoption. Individuals are more inclined to adopt cryptocurrency when their peers do, especially when the trading behavior of those peers aligns with their expressed opinions. When uncertainty or returns are high, investors tend to rely more on positive opinions, regardless of the actual trading behavior of others. We observe significant heterogeneity across user demographics: Users who are older, are more experienced, are more willing to take risks, and have higher incomes are especially responsive to credible signals from adopters. Younger users exhibit greater skepticism toward positive sentiment, possibly because of greater familiarity with noise in social media environments. Our results offer actionable insights for practitioners. Developers and marketers of emerging technologies can foster adoption by leveraging transparent, credible communication—preferably from peers whose actions are visible and aligned with their expressed opinions.</content>
